<span id="A2.T5.2.12.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:199.2pt;">
<span id="A2.T5.2.12.3.1.1" class="ltx_p">A standard six-sided fair die is rolled four times. The probability that the product of all four numbers rolled is a perfect square is <math id="A2.T5.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{m}{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{m}{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A2.T5.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.T5.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> are relatively prime positive integers. Find <math id="A2.T5.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m+n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m+n</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
